 Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite index built per kiosk from the active snapshot. Each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index when a signed snapshot arrives. The index file lives alongside the snapshot bundle on local storage and is queried with parameterized SQLite statements. No certificate behavior or interface change results from this selection.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records: build a staging SQLite index from the newline-delimited JSON records, verify the index contains exactly the validated records, then atomically replace the active index and snapshot metadata. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. Lookups are executed against the embedded SQLite index with a primary key on the normalized serial number, ensuring predictable sub-250 ms responses for 90,000 records on kiosk hardware.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index per kiosk | On-site HTTPS directory; Managed search service | Predictable lookups with no service to run; each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index on snapshot arrival, staying inside the registry component; avoids network dependency and recurring cost; certificate interface unchanged. |
+| Index rebuild timing | Rebuild on snapshot import, atomic swap | Incremental updates; lazy rebuild on first lookup | Guarantees a complete, validated index before activation; prevents partially imported records from being visible; aligns with HLD tenet that a failed import leaves last valid snapshot available. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Lookup backend selected as local embedded SQLite index.
+
+### Deferred
+1. Exact SQLite schema and indexing strategy to be finalized during implementation.
